# Line endings are LF in the repository and LF in every working copy, including Windows.
#
# Without this the repository has no opinion, so `core.autocrlf` decides — and it decides
# differently per machine. A developer with the Git for Windows default checks files out as
# CRLF; prettier's `endOfLine` defaults to `lf`; `prettier --check` then fails on every file
# it looks at. That is what happened here: 187 files red on CI, zero locally, because the
# local working copy happened to hold LF while a fresh checkout did not.
#
# `eol=lf` is the half that matters — it overrides `autocrlf` on checkout rather than leaving
# it to whatever each machine is configured with.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git must never touch the bytes. An icon or a database with line endings
# "normalised" into it is a corrupted file.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.db  binary
*.exe binary
